// @emit-error value-call union-ARM parameter given a value that is not that arm
// THE WITNESS THAT REFUTED AN EXEMPTION, kept because the refutation is the method.
//
// An `ObjLit` exemption was built into the arm-parameter floor beside this one, reasoning
// that `exprVariantIndex` has no object-literal arm by design and a bare `f({ r: 7 })`
// would be rejected needlessly. The evidence offered was "the corpus is byte-identical
// with the exemption in place" — and that evidence proved NOTHING, because the corpus
// contains no program in this family at all (D201). A population that could not have
// contained the disagreement cannot report its absence.
//
// Built as a program, this file is what the exemption did: on `1e81b0f3` it is a LOUD
// `emitProgram: ref valtype with no interned shape`; with the exemption it became
// `check-clean invalid wasm` — a cell falling INTO silence, which is the exact failure the
// floor exists to prevent. There is no exemption now: an argument that cannot be named as
// the arm is rejected, literal or not.
//
// NOTE THE SHAPE: no layout twin. `Circle` is the only `{r: i32}` in the program, so
// without the floor the struct scan answers -1 one stage later instead of matching a twin,
// and the disguise flips from loud to silent for that reason and no other.
type Circle = { r: i32 }
type Sq = { s: i32 }
type Shape = Circle | Sq
const f = (x: Circle) => x
print(f({ r: 7 }).r)
